  }))), "What problems remain and what are the next steps?"), mdx("p", null, "Based on the Conclusions and Future Work section of the paper (Unlocking Knowledge with OCR-Driven Document Digitization for Peruvian Indigenous Languages, AmericasNLP 2024):"), mdx("p", null, "\uD83D\uDEA7 Problems Remain (as stated by authors):"), mdx("p", null, "Scalability across languages: The current pipeline requires language-specific training data and community collaboration\u2014challenging to generalize to all Indigenous languages due to limited existing resources.\nSustained community engagement: Maintaining long-term partnerships with communities post-digitization to ensure ongoing validation and use of materials (e.g., avoiding \"digital abandonment\").\nTechnical limitations for non-Latin scripts: While improved for Quechua/Shipibo, complex scripts (e.g., tonal or logographic systems) still require specialized adaptations.\n\uD83D\uDD04 Next Steps (explicitly proposed by authors):"), mdx("p", null, "Expand to more Indigenous languages: Deploying the framework to Aymara, Moche, and other Peruvian languages using community-sourced materials.\nBuild a community validation toolkit: Creating open-source guidelines and templates to help communities independently verify OCR outputs.\nIntegrate with language preservation platforms: Partnering with initiatives like Digital Indigenous Languages to embed digitized texts into language learning apps or archives.\n\uD83D\uDCA1 Key quote from authors:\n\"We aim to move beyond a single language project by developing modular, community-adaptive workflows that can be rapidly deployed across the Americas\u2014prioritizing ethical scalability over technical perfection.\"\n(Source: AmericasNLP 2024, p. 110)"), mdx("p", null, "This shows the authors frame next steps as practical, community-driven extensions of their current work\u2014not theoretical idealism."), mdx("h5", {
