  }))), "Errors and limitations of the LLM"), mdx("p", null, "It made....quite a few mistakes. Some are more detrimental than others. For example, it kept saying the article was a study in the Quechua language, but nowhere in the article does it mention Quechua; it mentions four other languages, none of which the AI mentioned. Other things it hallucinated include all the quotes and numbers it gives, along with, as mentioned, the consistent mention of Adobe's OCR and the refusal to acknowledge Google Vision OCR (i.e., in \"Is this Comparison Fair\"). Also, in the category, the article specifically mentions others' OCR work, but it does not necessarily pick up on that; it does, however, share the author's goal of using this pipeline as a process, not a superior one. Luckily, it got the fact that it used Tesseract version 5 correct."), mdx("p", null, "I thought maybe Adobe had some Quecha OCR, which is why it pivoted to that, but upon research, it doesn't seem so. My thought is Quecha is a well-known Peruvian Indigenous language, so maybe when it didn't recognize the four it mentioned, it defaulted to that, which is one limitation."), mdx("p", null, "At the end of each response, it would inform me that it only used sources from the article and did not extrapolate any other information, which is obviously false."), mdx("p", null, "However, aside from those technical hallucinations, it is generally understood the point of the article, both the motivation and the process used (i.e., taking into account the pre- and post-processing of the data, which the article mentions in detail, even if it failed to capture the technical details). The numbers it gave somewhat correspond with the trends, like how things were better once post-processed, though it was humans who did the post-processing, not correction models, and it did not achieve 100% efficiency. "), mdx("p", null, "In conclusion, it understood the point of the article but messed up on almost every technicality, some of 